## 3. Advisory only (writes nothing) -- PASS

PROVEN, not read. Package-level import analysis is NOT sufficient here: the
`accounts` package as a whole imports `os` and `budget2/internal/services/
storage` (from `accounts.go`/A6's settings code), so `go list -deps` cannot
clear `Project`. I proved it at runtime instead.

I built a standalone binary in the scratch tree that calls `Project` once with
an anchor, two paired inbound transfers and a monthly recurring item, bracketed
by stdout markers, and ran it under strace tracing every file-creating and
file-writing syscall:

    $ strace -f -e trace=openat,open,creat,unlink,unlinkat,rename,renameat,\
      renameat2,mkdir,mkdirat,write,pwrite64,ftruncate ./probe-a5

    MARKER-BEFORE-PROJECT
    MARKER-AFTER-PROJECT
    err=<nil> available=true threshold=500 crossing=2026-09-01 minimum=400
      topup=100 ref=2000 hasref=true
    === EXIT=0 ===
    === any file opened for writing/creation? ===
      (none)
    === unlink/rename/mkdir/ftruncate calls? ===
      (none)
    === writes, with fd (1=stdout, 2=stderr) ===
    178082 write(1, "MARKER-BEFORE-PROJECT
    178082 write(1, "MARKER-AFTER-PROJECT
    178082 write(1, "err=<nil> available=true thresho"..., 102) = 102
    === files created in cwd during the run ===
    (empty directory)

Zero write-mode `openat`, zero `creat`, zero `unlink`/`rename`/`mkdir`/
`ftruncate`. The only `write` syscalls in the entire process are the probe's
own three fd-1 prints. No sidecar, no ledger write, no file of any kind.

Input mutation, by mutation testing:

    [M19 make Project sort the caller's anchors in place]  KILLED by:
        --- FAIL: TestProject_DoesNotMutateInputs
    [M20 make Project zero txs[0].Amount]                  KILLED by:
        --- FAIL: TestProject_ReferenceMedianOddCount
        --- FAIL: TestProject_ReferenceMedianEvenCount
        --- FAIL: TestProject_DoesNotMutateInputs

NOTE 1 (non-blocking, but it is a guard no test kills): a pure REORDER of the
caller's transaction slice is undetected.

    [M14 make Project sort txs in place]  SURVIVED -- no test killed this

The cause is fixture size, not a missing assertion:
`TestProject_DoesNotMutateInputs` (projection_test.go:416-418) builds `txs`
with exactly ONE element, so an in-place sort is a no-op. The test DOES have
the required length assertion (line 436) and DOES compare element-wise (line
439-443), so with n>=2 it would catch it. The implementation genuinely does not
reorder anything -- this is a weak test, not a defect. Worth a second
transaction in the fixture.

## 4. Unknown is not zero -- PASS (weighted; this is the one that moves money)

Source: projection.go:96-100 returns early on `!start.Available` with only
`AccountID`, `AsOf` and `Threshold` populated.

MUTATION -- I removed the guard so it projects from the zero `start.Amount`:

    [M1 unknown-becomes-zero: drop the !start.Available early return]
      KILLED by:
        --- FAIL: TestProject_NoAnchorIsUnavailableNotZero

The guard is real and a named test kills it.

The test fixture covers only "account has NO anchors at all". The more
insidious real-world shape is "account HAS an anchor, but it is dated after
asOf" (a future-dated export, or asOf before the first export). I probed that
case directly rather than assuming BalanceAt covers it:

    P1 anchor dated AFTER asOf      err=<nil> available=false threshold=500
                                    crossingZero=true minimum=0 topup=0
                                    ref=0 hasref=false

Correct: unavailable, nothing rolled forward, no fabricated number.

I also probed the OPPOSITE direction, which matters just as much -- a genuine
zero balance must still project, or "unknown" would have swallowed a real fact:

    P4 genuine zero balance         err=<nil> available=true threshold=500
                                    crossingZero=false minimum=-400
                                    topup=900 ref=0 hasref=false

An anchor of $0.00 is Available:true and projects to -400 with a $900 top-up.
Unknown and zero are discriminated in both directions. This item is sound.

