## 6. Suggested top-up rounded UP to the nearest $100 -- ***FAIL***

### The requirement

Brief item 6, verbatim: "**Suggested top-up** = shortfall rounded UP to the
nearest $100. Check an exact boundary (a shortfall of exactly 100 must not
become 200) and a value just past one."

### The code

projection.go:224-229:

    func roundUpToHundred(x float64) float64 {
        if x < floatTolerance {
            return 0
        }
        return math.Ceil(x/100) * 100
    }

Note that the negative-shortfall branch on the line above DOES use
`floatTolerance`. The ceil does not. Every other float comparison in this file
is epsilon-guarded -- projection.go:154 (`balance < out.Threshold -
floatTolerance`), projection.go:214 (`acct.LowBalanceThreshold >
floatTolerance`), projection.go:250 (`tx.Amount <= floatTolerance`). This one
line is the sole exception, which reads as an oversight rather than a design
choice.

### The defect

An unguarded `math.Ceil` turns any shortfall that is exactly a $100 multiple in
DECIMAL, but a hair above it in float64, into the next $100. Cent-valued bank
data produces exactly that dust. Twelve hand-built cases, each of which has a
decimal shortfall of exactly $100.00 (start - outflow = 400.00, threshold 500):

    start      outflow    minimum                  shortfall(=500-min)      topup
    800.10     400.10     400                      100                      100.00
    800.30     400.30     399.99999999999994       100.00000000000006       200.00  <== $200 for an exactly-$100 shortfall
    800.70     400.70     400.00000000000006       99.999999999999943       100.00
    1000.10    600.10     400                      100                      100.00
    900.20     500.20     400.00000000000006       99.999999999999943       100.00
    800.20     400.20     400.00000000000006       99.999999999999943       100.00
    2000.10    1600.10    400                      100                      100.00
    800.01     400.01     400                      100                      100.00
    800.29     400.29     399.99999999999994       100.00000000000006       200.00  <== $200 for an exactly-$100 shortfall
    1200.30    800.30     400                      100                      100.00
    700.70     300.70     400.00000000000006       99.999999999999943       100.00
    600.60     200.60     400                      100                      100.00

$800.30 with a $400.30 monthly bill is entirely ordinary data.

Swept over all 100 cent values, and again with five weekly occurrences so the
dust accumulates:

    cent sweep: 24 of 100 exactly-$100 shortfalls were rounded to $200
    first offender: start=800.04 outflow=400.04 minimum=399.99999999999994 topup=200.00
    5-occurrence weekly sweep: 40 of 100 rounded to $200 instead of $100

24% and 40%. Not a corner case.

### Why the existing tests miss it

`TestProject_TopUpRoundingAtExactBoundary` (projection_test.go:203-221) uses
800 - 400, and `TestRoundUpToHundred` (line 505-525) feeds
`roundUpToHundred` the integer literals 100, 200, 101, 199, 250, 1. Every one
of those is exactly representable in binary floating point, so the boundary is
asserted only where it cannot break. The test proves the algebra, not the
arithmetic.

### Proof this is a real gap and not a behavior something pins

I applied the one-line fix in the scratch tree and re-ran the full accounts
suite:

    [FIX-CHECK epsilon-guarded ceil: math.Ceil(x/100-floatTolerance)*100]
      SURVIVED -- no test killed this mutation

Not one existing test distinguishes the fixed version from the broken one --
the boundary requirement is entirely untested on the axis where it fails. And
the same fix removes the defect completely:

    cent sweep: 0 of 100 exactly-$100 shortfalls were rounded to $200
    5-occurrence weekly sweep: 0 of 100 rounded to $200 instead of $100

### Impact

This number tells the user how many real dollars to move between real bank
accounts. Over-recommending by $100 can overdraw the SOURCE account. "Advisory"
is not a defense: a figure presented as "move $200" when $100 suffices is
authoritative and wrong, which is the precise failure class this projection
exists to avoid.

The opposite direction is not reachable: dust is ~1e-13, far below one cent, so
a genuine $100.01 shortfall still correctly rounds to $200 (probe P5, below).
The failure is one-directional and always an over-recommendation.

Correct behavior IS produced when the arithmetic is float-exact, end to end:

    P5 outflow 400.00 -> shortfall 100.00   minimum=400     topup=100
    P5 outflow 400.01 -> shortfall 100.01   minimum=399.99  topup=200

Both halves of item 6's stated check pass on exact values and the first half
fails on cent values.

### Suggested repair (for the worker, not applied by me)

    return math.Ceil(x/100-floatTolerance) * 100

plus a regression test using cent-valued fixtures -- e.g. start 800.30, monthly
outflow 400.30, threshold 500, expect SuggestedTopUp == 100.

