## 8. Median inbound paired transfer -- PASS (with note)

`medianInboundPairedTransfer` (projection.go:238-265). Each filter, mutated:

    [M8  drop the paired-class filter]              KILLED by
        TestProject_ReferenceNoPriorTransfers
    [M9  drop the account filter]                   KILLED by
        TestProject_ReferenceNoPriorTransfers
    [M10 drop the inbound (positive amount) filter] KILLED by
        TestProject_ReferenceNoPriorTransfers
    [M11 drop the TransactionType==Transfer filter] SURVIVED
    [M12 even count returns upper-middle not mean]  KILLED by
        TestProject_ReferenceMedianEvenCount
    [M13 report zero as a number instead of absent] KILLED by
        TestProject_ReferenceNoPriorTransfers

Odd count: `TestProject_ReferenceMedianOddCount` asserts 2000 from
{1000,2000,3000}. Even count: `TestProject_ReferenceMedianEvenCount` asserts
2250 = (2000+2500)/2, and M12 proves the mean-of-two-middle branch is pinned.
"No history" is `HasReference=false` with amount 0, and M13 proves the absent
flag is what is asserted, not the zero.

The even-count median divides in full precision (projection.go:264) with no
intermediate rounding -- item 9 satisfied here.

NOTE 2 (non-blocking, guard no test kills): M11 survived. The
`tx.TransactionType != models.Transfer` check (projection.go:241) is redundant
given the `TransferClass != "paired"` check on the next line, because a row can
only acquire `TransferClass = ClassPaired` inside
`transfers.pairLegs` (transfers.go:395-403), which sets
`TransactionType = models.Transfer` on the same two lines. So no reachable
input distinguishes the two checks and no test can kill M11. Defensive
redundancy, not a defect -- but it is untested, so I flag it.

NOTE 3 (non-blocking): projection.go:244 hard-codes the string literal
`"paired"` rather than referencing `transfers.ClassPaired` (transfers.go:53).
Renaming that constant would silently make the reference amount vanish with no
compile error and no failing test. The brief spells the requirement with the
literal ("TransferClass == \"paired\""), so A5 is spec-compliant; noting the
coupling for the lead.

## 9. Float discipline -- PASS

`grep -n "==\|!=" internal/services/accounts/projection.go` returns eight
hits, and NONE compares a float64:

    93:  if err != nil                          (error)
    178: if leg.AccountID == accountID          (string)
    234: (comment)
    241: if tx.TransactionType != models.Transfer  (string-kinded type)
    244: if tx.TransferClass != "paired"        (string)
    247: if tx.AccountID != acct.ID             (string)
    255: if len(amounts) == 0                   (int)
    260: if n%2 == 1                            (int)

Every money comparison uses `floatTolerance` (= 1e-9, balance.go:16). Tests use
`moneyEq` (balance_test.go:15-17, `math.Abs(a-b) < floatTolerance`).

`projection_test.go:432` and `:440` do use exact `!=` on amounts / whole
Transaction structs, but those are the mutation guards in
`TestProject_DoesNotMutateInputs`, where bit-for-bit equality is the CORRECT
assertion -- an unchanged input must be byte-identical, not merely close. Not a
violation.

Rounding appears in exactly one place, `roundUpToHundred` -- the deliberate
$100 step. No intermediate rounding anywhere in the roll-forward. This
requirement is satisfied as stated; item 6 fails on the SEPARATE point that
that one rounding lacks an epsilon.

