## Merge note -- VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY, no behavioral difference

I did not take this on trust. The A5 worktree still exists at
`/tmp/swarm-worktrees/budget2/work/A5` (branch `work/A5`, HEAD ff89ce2, i.e.
before A3's e9ad6ae landed). Its `git status` shows
`M internal/models/transaction.go` -- A5's dropped copy.

`diff -u <merged>/internal/models/transaction.go <A5-worktree>/...` shows the
differences are ENTIRELY comments, const-block gofmt alignment, and field
ORDER within the struct. The load-bearing declarations are identical in both:

    Transfer TransactionType = "Transfer"          (same identifier, same value)
    TransferClass   string `json:"transfer_class,omitempty"`
    TransferPairKey string `json:"transfer_pair_key,omitempty"`

A5's own doc comment on TransferClass names the same value vocabulary --
"paired" / "external" / "" -- as A3's. So no A5 behavior depended on a
differing definition: the type of `TransferClass` is `string` in both, the
constant `Transfer` carries the same underlying value in both, and
`projection.go` compares against the literal `"paired"` which is
`transfers.ClassPaired` in both. Field reordering within a struct is
irrelevant to `projection.go`, which addresses fields by name only.

Confirming the merge took A5's two files verbatim:

    $ diff -u <merged>/internal/services/accounts/projection.go \
              <A5-worktree>/internal/services/accounts/projection.go
    P_EXIT=0   (identical)
    $ diff -u <merged>/.../projection_test.go <A5-worktree>/.../projection_test.go
    T_EXIT=0   (identical)

And the merged tree builds, vets, tests and staticchecks clean (item 2), which
is the mechanical confirmation that `projection.go` compiles against A3's
definitions.

Conclusion: the merge is coherent. This is NOT the cause of the item-6 failure,
which is present identically in A5's own worktree.

## Ruling 2026-08-16e -- loop assertions

Ruling text (ACCOUNTS_TRANSFERS_SPEC.md:122-129): "Any loop-based probe
assertion must be preceded by a count assertion", the rationale being that it
"passes trivially on an empty set".

Two loops in `projection_test.go` lack a preceding count assertion. Both
iterate a slice built from an in-function literal, so neither can be vacuous on
an empty set and neither triggers the ruling's actual hazard. Flagged for
completeness as instructed:

NOTE 4a: `TestRoundUpToHundred` (projection_test.go:519) loops over `cases`, an
8-element literal declared four lines above. No `len(cases)` assertion.
Cannot be empty.

NOTE 4b: `TestProject_DoesNotMutateInputs` (projection_test.go:431) loops
`for i := range anchorsBefore` and indexes `acct.Anchors[i]` without asserting
`len(acct.Anchors) == len(anchorsBefore)` first -- it DOES make that assertion
for `txs` (line 436) and `recurring` (line 444), so the omission on anchors is
an inconsistency. Indexing is safe here regardless (the caller's slice header
is a value copy, so its length cannot change), and the fixture is a 2-element
literal.

Neither is a defect. Both would be improved by a one-line count assertion.

## Verdict

FAIL on requirement 6.

The projection is otherwise well built: the two failure modes I was told to
weight -- projecting from an assumed zero when the balance is unknown, and
another account's bills bleeding into this forecast -- are both correctly
implemented AND both killed by named tests under mutation (M1, M2, M2b), and I
confirmed the unknown/zero distinction holds in both directions and on the
harder "anchor dated after asOf" shape the tests do not cover.

But the suggested top-up is the number the user acts on, requirement 6 named
the exact boundary to protect, and that boundary is wrong for 24-40% of
cent-valued inputs while no test can tell the broken version from the fixed
one. A user with an $800.30 balance and a $400.30 bill is told to move $200
when $100 is correct. That is a one-line fix plus one regression test; it
should be a fast attempt 2.
